<a2ui>
$/={battery_comparison:[{chemistry:"LFP",energy_density:"Lower than NMC; IEA says LFP packs are about one-fifth lower by mass and one-third lower by volume than NMC packs [22]",cycle_life:"Typically long; sources cite roughly 2,000–6,000+ cycles, with some BESS-grade cells higher [3][18]",safety:"Strong; better thermal stability and lower thermal-runaway risk than NMC",cost:"Lower; IEA says LFP packs were almost 30% cheaper per kWh than NMC in 2025, and more than 40% cheaper on average in 2025 data[21]",maturity:"Mature, widely deployed in EVs and especially stationary storage",maturity_note:""},{chemistry:"NMC",energy_density:"Higher; generally the best of the three for compactness and range, with IEA confirming the energy-density advantage over LFP",cycle_life:"Moderate; commonly around 1,000–3,000 cycles, varying by design and usage [4][7]",safety:"Good but weaker than LFP; high-nickel versions have greater thermal-risk sensitivity",cost:"Higher; IEA reports LFP is substantially cheaper per kWh than NMC",maturity:"Very mature and mainstream in EVs, especially where range and weight matter most",maturity_note:""},{chemistry:"Sodium-ion",energy_density:"Lower than lithium-ion; commercial ranges are often around 90–160 Wh/kg, though some newer cells are higher [5][6]",cycle_life:"Promising; commercial ranges are broad, roughly 500–8,000 cycles depending on chemistry and duty cycle",safety:"Good; generally regarded as safer and more thermally stable than many lithium-ion chemistries",cost:"Potentially attractive, but current pricing is still variable; IRENA and industry sources place it roughly in the same order as or above today’s LFP in many cases[14]",maturity:"Emerging; gaining traction, but still less mature than LFP and NMC",maturity_note:""}]}
root=Column([a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l],gap="md",padding=16)
a=Text("Battery comparison","h2")
b=Table(columns=[{key:"chemistry",label:"Chemistry"},{key:"energy_density",label:"Energy density"},{key:"cycle_life",label:"Cycle life"},{key:"safety",label:"Safety"},{key:"cost",label:"Cost"},{key:"maturity",label:"Maturity"},{key:"maturity_note",label:"Maturity Note"}],statePath="/battery_comparison",domain="comparison",preferredPresentation="cards",primaryColumn="chemistry")
c=Text("Best-fit uses","h2")
d=Text("For EVs, NMC is best when maximum range, lower pack weight, and compact packaging are priorities, such as premium cars and long-range models. LFP is better for cost-sensitive EVs, fleet vehicles, and urban cars where safety, durability, and lower lifetime cost matter more than peak energy density. Sodium-ion is a good fit for short-range EVs, cold-climate use cases, and low-cost mobility, but its lower energy density still limits it in long-range vehicles.",variant="body")
e=Text("For stationary storage, LFP is usually the default choice because its long cycle life, strong safety profile, and lower cost align well with daily cycling and large-scale systems. Sodium-ion is also promising for grid storage and backup power, especially where raw energy density matters less than safety, supply-chain resilience, and cost potential. NMC is generally less attractive for stationary storage unless a project specifically needs higher energy density in a constrained footprint.",variant="body")
f=Text("Practical takeaway","h2")
g=Text("If you need the safest and most economical mainstream option, choose LFP. If you need the highest range in the smallest battery, choose NMC. If you want an emerging lower-risk chemistry for storage or lower-cost EVs, sodium-ion is promising but still less mature.",variant="body")
</a2ui>